How the franchise model shapes this build. Unit franchisees pay a 10 percent royalty on gross revenue plus a 1.5 percent advertising fee in the 2025 FDD summary, and the regional office supplies initial accounts and support. That has three consequences: the owner's margin is tight, so call-out coverage that protects a contract matters; the brand belongs to Jani-King International, so use of its name, logo and training claims needs the franchisor's sign-off; and the regional office may already offer training or tools the build should not duplicate.

The pitch and design rules
The site is not a brochure. It is the owner's assistant: it fills open jobs, covers call-outs at school and hospital accounts, trains the crew, and sells services, while the owner stays on his phone for the few decisions only he can make.

Three things win the room, in this order:

Hiring fast lane. Friend-referred applicants land on his phone as a one-tap card. No reference calls.

The Bench. When someone calls out, the next person is already being texted and the school or hospital contact barely notices. In a franchise paying 10 percent royalty on gross, a lost account hurts twice.

The Assistant. One thing on his phone that knows the whole business and does what he says.

Supporting pieces: free, credible training with proof on file; English and Spanish everywhere; crew recognition; client-facing service pages.

