Module A: Hiring fast lane
The flow is unchanged from v0.1, with two changes for school and hospital work. The market context is part-time night cleaning: the one local unit listing found asks for night-shift availability in Youngsville, so shift fit and transportation are the first things the card should show.

A worker or friend texts an apply link. Each worker has a personal referral link; there is also a general link for the Apply page.

The applicant fills a 2-minute form on their phone: first name, mobile number, area, shifts available (days, nights, weekends), has transportation (yes/no), languages (English/Spanish), who told you about us (a crew member or "nobody"), and consent to receive texts. Save-and-resume, no account.

The owner gets a push notification and a card: details, who vouched for them, and that person's record with the company (tenure, attendance).

The owner taps Hire, Pass, Later, or Ask her to screen (the assistant texts three short questions and updates the card).

Hire sends the welcome text: start details, first-shift contact and the Rookie Run training link. Pass sends a polite optional message. Later sets a reminder.

If he prefers to call, the card has a call button and the system still logs the outcome.

Why it kills reference calls. The crew member who referred the applicant is the reference, and the card shows their track record.

New in v0.2: a site-eligibility flag. Schools and hospitals commonly require contractor staff to clear screening before they set foot on site. The system still does not run background checks or drug tests, which stay in the owner's existing process. Instead the card carries a manual "cleared for schools / cleared for hospitals" toggle the owner sets, and the Bench only offers those sites to workers with the matching flag. Confirm the actual requirements in the owner's school and hospital contracts before launch.

Extras: nudges when an applicant stalls mid-form; an optional referral bonus (amount set by the owner) released after 30 days and tracked automatically; a referral leaderboard that feeds the recognition wall.

Module B: The Bench (call-out coverage)
This is still the biggest-value piece, and school and hospital accounts make it sharper. Every site has a primary worker and two backups, plus a floater pool. A missed night at a hospital or a school before morning classes is the kind of failure that puts a contract at risk.

